{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} |''^```|engine|hub|Hub|must|Must'\"' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Director’s build plan\n7:## 1. The game to make\n13:The presentation is the engine’s shaded, code-drawn pixel art made specific to insects: luminous wings, striped segmented bodies, springy antennae, odd little clockwork gardeners, waxy husks, jewel-li [... omitted end of long line]\n15:Deliver a complete **six-encounter campaign, approximately 10–12 minutes on a successful run**, with an ending, replay, and a local best score. Leave headroom for Hub’s 15-minute hard session cap. Inc [... omitted end of long line]\n17:### Title ownership\n19:The builder must silently generate **50 distinct playful riffs on the source’s name**, then select one with an actual random-number generator, such as `crypto.randomInt(50)` or Python `secrets.randbel [... omitted end of long line]\n21:### Pillars to protect\n23:1. **A swarm you can feel.** Steering, compression, stinging, losses, and replenishment must be obvious through motion and sound.\n29:## 2. Research and fidelity\n50:## 3. Combat contract\n52:### Player swarm\n61:Focus is the one substantial new player verb. It must create a visible and useful decision. Do not add a projectile gun, inventory, skill tree, crafting loop, or unrelated dash economy.\n63:Use an authoritative swarm footprint for contact, informed by formation and surviving-bee count. Render the actual number of surviving bees around that footprint. The visible extent, optional contact  [... omitted end of long line]\n65:### Stinging targets\n69:Show damage with a changing body pose and a small local progress treatment, rather than a permanent forest of health bars. Interrupting an attack must clearly show recovery. The last sting produces a  [... omitted end of long line]\n73:### Hostile swarms\n81:Contact removes bees at a controlled damage cadence. Overlapping several enemies must not empty twelve bees in one update. Give a short, clearly communicated damage grace period, an unmistakable lost- [... omitted end of long line]\n83:Stage enemies visibly in entrance cells before release. Do not spawn on a player, inside a solid husk, or without warning. Decorative entrances may be elaborate; the arrival cue must remain clear.\n85:### Horizontal ray\n91:### Arena-changing husks\n93:Fallen automatons leave static obstacles for the rest of the encounter. Their footprint is established at the kill location with a slight visible settle. Both the obstruction and its edges must be obv [... omitted end of long line]\n97:### Pacing, score, failure\n103:In solo, losing the final bee fails the encounter. In co-op, a fallen swarm becomes a clearly marked observer until the surviving player clears the encounter; both return at the next checkpoint. Losin [... omitted end of long line]\n107:### Initial tuning, to be tested rather than treated as truth\n109:Express movement and geometry in engine-native world units; one engine tile is 16 units. Begin with an arena around 24 by 15 tiles and adjust its framing before dressing it.\n125:## 4. Campaign and content tools\n144:Developer stage tools must let a future designer select/restart an encounter, change its seed, inspect collision and spawn zones, and tune the important variables without rewriting game logic. Keep co [... omitted end of long line]\n146:## 5. Presentation and animation\n148:### Composition\n150:Use a fixed **`overhead`** engine view. The engine’s `topdown` view is tilted; exact overhead makes row attacks and collision truthful. Depth comes from native shaded volumes, ground shadows, wings, e [... omitted end of long line]\n152:Explicitly fit the projected arena bounds plus a safety margin inside a gameplay rectangle that excludes the HUD and touch controls. Use the tighter width/height fit through the engine’s supported cam [... omitted end of long line]\n158:### Native animation foundation\n160:Extend the engine’s procedural rigs rather than importing a different animation stack. `E.Blob` supplies squash, eyes, and wing flapping; its state-driven `update`/`draw` pattern is a suitable foundat [... omitted end of long line]\n162:Required animation states: idle hover, acceleration/bank, Focus compression, contact stinging, target recovery, warning/maturation, damage recoil, bee loss, charged/empty ray, target collapse, swarm r [... omitted end of long line]\n164:Use native custom shapes and materials for fixed scenery, native pixel sprites/animations for tiny icons or spark accents, and custom rigs for moving anatomy. Preserve the engine’s pixel edges, palett [... omitted end of long line]\n166:### Audio, entirely written in code\n168:Use engine synthesis/Web Audio for all sound. Do not ship audio files, recorded dialogue, encoded samples, or downloaded music. Create distinct procedural voices for calm wings, agitated wings, stingi [... omitted end of long line]\n170:Write a light original arcade garden score with layers that react to pressure and a short finale resolution. **Music defaults to 10%** on first use. Wire that default into the engine’s actual persiste [... omitted end of long line]\n172:Occasional tiny Animalese/Simlish-like chatter gives the garden characters personality. Generate syllable envelopes and pitches in code; use no intelligible sampled speech. Start sound after a permiss [... omitted end of long line]\n174:## 6. Controller-first screens and input\n176:Use the engine’s input and menu infrastructure. Extend native pause/settings/controls rather than duplicating them with a separate web UI. Every screen must work with a standard-mapped DualSense, keyb [... omitted end of long line]\n187:Use action mappings, not scattered hardware codes. Offer rebinding through native controls. Normalize diagonal movement, apply dead zones, and clear held gameplay input at transitions. A held button f [... omitted end of long line]\n191:For touch, keep only the menu button and a charged ray button when useful. The movement stick is invisible; a brief first-use gesture hint may fade. Handle simultaneous movement, Focus hold, and ray t [... omitted end of long line]\n193:Required screens are Title, a short Play/Co-op choice, Settings/Controls, About/Credits, Pause with Developer tools, encounter result, failure/retry, and ending. Use large native menu rows and compact [... omitted end of long line]\n195:The gameplay HUD needs only team score, encounter progress, player markers/bee counts, and charged/empty status. Show short contextual teaching during safe early moments, then get out of the way. Keep [... omitted end of long line]\n197:### Attract mode\n201:Any input exits attract to the title, consumes that input, and restarts the idle timer. Cover mouse movement, analogue movement, keyboard, button presses, and touch—not only clicks. Suppress controlle [... omitted end of long line]\n203:## 7. Engine integration and source organization\n209:- [Handoff README](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/README.md)\n210:- [API card](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/API.md) and [API index](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353 [... omitted end of long line]\n211:- [Native asset authoring](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md)\n212:- [Arcade module](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/modules/arcade.js)\n214:Vendor the handoff into `vendor/engine/`. Prefer the full dependency-free `my-3d2dge.js`; it already contains modules, so do not register them twice. Adapt the useful arcade shell rather than retainin [... omitted end of long line]\n218:```text\n224:src/net/                    Hub, direct peer, shared authority/packet contract\n227:vendor/engine/              required handoff and notices\n228:vendor/hub/                 pristine SDK and test harness dependencies\n233:research.md                 verified source/engine/network evidence\n234:```\n236:Change module boundaries when the actual source suggests a better design. Keep dependency direction clear: simulation knows no renderer, DOM, audio device, or transport. Presentation consumes snapshot [... omitted end of long line]\n238:### Verified extension points\n248:These are orientation points, not a substitute for reading the implementation. Keep the complete relevant API cards, examples, engine implementation, Hub contract, and acceptance ledger in working con [... omitted end of long line]\n250:### Simulation discipline\n252:Use a seeded RNG and the engine’s fixed update cadence; its headless stepping is 1/120 second. Do not use wall time, `Math.random()`, frame count from rendering, or audio completion for gameplay. Esta [... omitted end of long line]\n254:Keep compact serializable authoritative state: seed/tick, phase, encounter, players, target state, rival state, husks, score, charge, timers, and concluding-target phase. Cosmetic wings and particles  [... omitted end of long line]\n256:Preserve a fixed gameplay arena under resize and native `expand` behavior. Scene changes must clear stale timers, input, effects, and developer time scale; native scene changes do not necessarily rese [... omitted end of long line]\n258:## 8. Multiplayer that actually works\n260:Use **one host-authoritative shared world for 1–2 players**. The network path must preserve meaningful player steering and actions. A remote player cannot merely watch a host simulation.\n262:The planning pass inspected **AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0**, commit `35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869`:\n264:- [Protocol/API](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/README.md)\n265:- [Pristine session client](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/session.js)\n266:- [Shared-world example](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/examples/arena.html)\n267:- [Harness](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/harness.html) and [automated checker](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2 [... omitted end of long line]\n271:### Hub lifecycle\n274:2. Call `AIHub.connect({mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,2]})`. A null result selects the standalone flow.\n275:3. When active, build the world and automatically call `AIHub.ready()` once. Bypass title, attract, local join, and blocking start screens. Use Hub’s instruction card.\n276:4. Respect its start clock. The start event arrives before play: gameplay input and simulation remain gated until `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n277:5. Only the host advances gameplay. The host may be a dedicated server copy with `AIHub.server === true` and no local player. Never assume `AIHub.me` occupies a seat or that authority has a canvas, au [... omitted end of long line]\n278:6. Handle leave, time-up, and end. Neutralize departed inputs; if a guest leaves, the remaining player can continue. A host departure is a void round managed by Hub. On time-up, host finishes inside t [... omitted end of long line]\n279:7. Host calls `AIHub.finish(...)` exactly once, supplying valid scores, win/loss, and a cooperative tied standing for the remaining player IDs according to the current result schema. Campaign completi [... omitted end of long line]\n281:Declare a duration appropriate to the finished campaign rather than a sample game’s short timer. The inspected Hub runtime enforces a **15-minute hard cap**, even if longer duration metadata is suppli [... omitted end of long line]\n283:While Hub is active, use **only its transport**. Do not create WebRTC, WebSocket, EventSource, public broker requests, chat, emotes, custom-name entry, new windows, dialogs, or navigation. Display sup [... omitted end of long line]\n285:### Transport-independent authority\n287:Normalize local pad, keyboard, touch, Hub input, and direct-peer input into one command format. Validate sender ownership, finite/ranged axes, phase, sequence, and monotonic action counters. A guest s [... omitted end of long line]\n289:Start with approximately 15 guest input packets/s and 12 host snapshots/s, reserving headroom for reliable phase and result events. Hub permits **20 outbound game-data messages/s**, burst 40, and at m [... omitted end of long line]\n293:Use interpolation for remote presentation and gentle local movement prediction/reconciliation. Host authority decides contact and damage. Use action counters or equivalent acknowledgements so loss can [... omitted end of long line]\n297:### Standalone play\n304:- **Direct peer:** a minimal manual WebRTC offer/answer exchange, available only outside an active Hub session. One peer creates a connection offer after ICE gathering; the other accepts it and return [... omitted end of long line]\n306:Give direct setup a short step-by-step screen with Copy, Paste, connection status, Cancel, retry, and malformed-code recovery. Clipboard failure must leave selectable/pasteable text and a working fall [... omitted end of long line]\n310:## 9. Developer mode and inspectability\n314:Expose the tuning table’s important variables as **live sliders** with units and **Reset tuning to defaults**. Update the authoritative tuning model at a safe tick boundary. Restart-only changes must  [... omitted end of long line]\n318:In standalone peer play, only the host can change authoritative developer settings; replicate any permitted change and mark both results assisted. In Hub, keep gameplay modification disabled as descri [... omitted end of long line]\n320:## 10. Execution order and integration gates\n322:Use the long context window to keep the actual engine, API, source evidence, content definitions, and integration contracts coherent throughout the build. Maintain a compact acceptance ledger with eac [... omitted end of long line]\n324:### Gate A — Prove the foundations\n326:Read required instructions and handoff documentation. Select the title properly. Vendor and record engine/Hub versions and notices. Establish the single-file build early. Boot a native overhead scene, [... omitted end of long line]\n330:### Gate B — Make the central minute satisfying\n336:### Gate C — Finish shared-world control\n338:Integrate two local swarms, player-count scaling, authoritative snapshots, guest actions, loss/jitter handling, dedicated server, leave/end rules, and actual direct-peer setup. Run the real Hub harnes [... omitted end of long line]\n342:### Gate D — Complete the campaign\n348:### Gate E — Finish the presentation and access paths\n354:### Gate F — Ship and verify the actual artifact\n360:## 11. Verification plan\n362:Keep all automated checks behind **`node tools/test.mjs`**, exiting nonzero on failure. Routine output is only actionable failures and one final count, for example `148 checks: 148 passed, 0 failed`.  [... omitted end of long line]\n366:### Deterministic simulation and content\n370:Run an action-driven bot through **all six encounters** using normal movement/formation/ray commands. It may inspect world state to choose actions; the principal completion test must not teleport, gra [... omitted end of long line]\n372:Use `new E.Game({headless:true,draw:true})`, `game.advance`, and injectable input. Inspect collected errors. Native kit tests and `vendor/engine/tools/check.mjs` can inform the wrapper; adapt their no [... omitted end of long line]\n374:### Browser, input, storage, and audio\n376:Test the packaged file in installed Chromium and WebKit. Probe Firefox’s no-WebGL environment for graceful operation as applicable to the Canvas engine. Do not claim physical-console or real-iOS verif [... omitted end of long line]\n382:Check attract at just before and after 10 seconds, its feature coverage, cancellation by every input family, and isolation of scores/settings/run state. Verify that Hub never enters attract.\n384:### Real multiplayer harness\n388:Drive the harness with one and two players, then the two-player latency profile of approximately **150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% loss**. Include guest departure, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=se [... omitted end of long line]\n390:The official checker is invoked from its own repository as `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <served-game-path> --players 2`; adapt its harness into the project runner or drive it through the installed Pyth [... omitted end of long line]\n394:### Targeted visual inspection\n396:Use screenshots only for what state checks cannot establish: title identity, 10-foot legibility, native-style composition, swarm/hostile distinction, Focus readability, contact and recovery cues, ray  [... omitted end of long line]\n398:### Shared-machine discipline\n402:## 12. Packaging, provenance, and completion\n404:Build a single HTML artifact at the exact name/destination specified in your brief. Inline engine, game code, content, CSS, and any required drawn assets so standalone play needs no runtime local fetc [... omitted end of long line]\n406:Test opening the final artifact directly through `file://`, and the same bytes through loopback for Hub. No absolute workstation paths, missing development assets, external audio, broken source-map de [... omitted end of long line]\n408:The in-game About/Credits and final chat reply must contain **Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback**, with every field/question required by your own brief. Popula [... omitted end of long line]\n410:Include the real source, this plan’s filename, actual chosen genre/perspective, actual engine/library versions, authored art/animation/audio approach, functioning online features, research performed,  [... omitted end of long line]\n412:Track and answer every required testing, engine, and prompt feedback question. Explain actual engine changes and why; write none if unchanged. Catalogue the game as a 1–2 player Action game with accur [... omitted end of long line]\n414:### Final acceptance ledger\n417:- [ ] Required engine is the functioning foundation for visuals, animation, input, menus, and game loop.\n421:- [ ] Solo, two-player couch, actual standalone direct-peer, Hub shared-world, and dedicated no-local-player authority all work.\n422:- [ ] Hub’s metadata, pristine SDK placement, start clock, traffic limits, leave/time-up/end, and valid once-only results pass its real harness.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
